            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Darkness primarily symbolizes moral and spiritual evil, sin, ignorance, death, and separation from God, functioning as the antithesis of divine light and often representing the domain of Satan.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Darkness symbolizes concealed disobedience, specifically denoting evil deeds that are intentionally hidden from the light.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Moral/spiritual evil, sin, and alienation from God in general.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Concealed disobedience (hidden sin).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus views darkness as a broad symbol for all spiritual evil, blindness, systemic opposition to God, and judgment. The book restricts the definition specifically to the act of concealing disobedience and hiding deeds from the light.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no direct contradiction, as concealed disobedience is a recognized aspect of biblical evil (particularly in John 3), but the book's definition excludes broader existential, cosmic, or systemic uses of darkness (e.g., 'domain of darkness', spiritual blindness, or eschatological judgment).\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book takes a specific mechanism of evil mentioned in the consensus (sinful deeds hiding from the light, based on John 3:19-20) and elevates it to the primary definition, thereby narrowing the symbol's extension from general evil/ignorance to hidden disobedience.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"While John 3:19-20 describes men loving darkness to hide their evil deeds, other passages use darkness to describe an overarching spiritual state, a cosmic domain (Colossians 1:13), or general ignorance of God (1 Peter 2:9), which goes beyond merely the concealment of specific disobedient acts.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
